    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "content": "Arrays and Pointers"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "In our last example, we saw that the array name can also decay to a pointer."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Thus, if we have an array numbers[], then the array name numbers can be used as a pointer that points to the first element of the array."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "For example,"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "content": "1#include <iostream>\n2using namespace std;\n3\n4int main() {\n5\n6    // array of numbers\n7    int numbers[5] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};\n8  \n9    // address of first array element\n10    cout << &numbers[0] << endl;    // 0x7ffef078f350\n11  \n12    // address of first array element\n13    cout << numbers << endl;    // 0x7ffef078f350\n14\n15    return 0;\n16}"
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "alt": "Idea Emoji",
      "src": "data:image/png;base64,"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Now, we can use this pointer to access elements of the array."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Here,"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "1. &numbers[0] is equivalent to numbers. Hence, the first element 1 can be accessed using *numbers."
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "alt": "&numbers[0] is equivalent to numbers. Hence, the first element 1 can be accessed using *numbers.",
      "src": "data:image/png;base64,"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "2. &numbers[1] is equivalent to numbers + 1 and the second element 2 can be accessed using *(numbers + 1)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "3. &numbers[2] is equivalent to numbers + 2 and the third element 3 can be accessed using *(numbers + 2)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "And, so on..."
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "alt": "&numbers[i] is equivalent to (numbers + i) and numbers[i] is equivalent to *(numbers + i) and so on.",
      "src": "data:image/png;base64,"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Basically,"
    },
    {
      "type": "bullet_list",
      "items": [
        "&numbers[i] is equivalent to numbers + i",
        "numbers[i] is equivalent to *(numbers + i)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Now, let's implement this in a working example."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "content": "How does it work?"
    },
